tweets.full_text:
@BitPaine That’s a broad concern among institutions right now.

They’re not super technical and see things breaking, so they reasonably wonder if bitcoin can break too, and as risk manages they correctly assign a limit.

What gets interesting months from now is if it doesn’t break.

Institutions consider expected value analysis.

You consider a tail risk (e.g. Bitcoin’s core cryptography gets rekt) and you assign it a percentage, maybe 1%.

New information comes in, so you say 5% now. Could be 2 or 3 or 5, you don’t know. But you and your quants run a billion dollars. You then re-run the average bull/base/bear numbers with this bigger bear outlier that potentially zeroes the whole thing, and come in with a lower buy price, or allocation size.

